        "flows": [
          "Stream request: POST /api/assistant-reply -> route decodes body with a route-local schema (400 validation) -> server/identity.ts authenticates (401/403) -> stream.ts openAssistantReplyStream({command, actor, requestSignal}) -> open-reply-stream.ts creates the single AbortController and links request.signal -> await generateReply(command, {model, policy, clock}, controller.signal) -> transport acquires the upstream stream and validates the response envelope.",
          "Pre-commit outcome: typed failure returns {kind:'refused', code}, listener removed and controller aborted -> route maps code to 400/401/403/429/503/500 with a safe JSON body; no Response exists yet, so nothing is committed.",
          "Commit: {kind:'open', body} -> route returns new Response(body, {status:200, headers: text/event-stream, no-store no-transform, x-accel-buffering:no}); commit happens only in the route.",
          "Post-commit pump: each ReplyDelta -> chunk frame and the idle timer is cleared and re-armed, so a long healthy stream never trips it and there is no total cap; normal end -> done frame and close.",
          "Idle timeout: timer fires -> controller.abort(IdleTimeout) -> provider observes the abort -> pump catch emits in-band {type:'error', code:'unavailable'} and closes the stream at status 200.",
          "Cancellation: browser abort or downstream cancel -> ReadableStream cancel(reason) -> controller.abort(reason) -> upstream fetch/SDK aborts, idle timer cleared, abort listener removed, provider reader released, and no report is emitted because a disconnect is not a defect.",
          "Post-commit failure reporting: expected semantic failure -> in-band error event with its code; unexpected exception -> reporter.capture once with requestId/traceId/actorId plus an already-reported marker so onRequestError does not double-capture -> in-band {type:'error', code:'unknown'} -> close. The stream is never error()-ed with raw provider text.",
          "Browser: client/use-reply-stream.ts POSTs with its own AbortController, reads frames, appends chunk text, ends on done, renders error.code as terminal state, and aborts on stop/unmount which reaches the server as cancel().",
          "Job: queue delivery -> app/api/jobs/assistant-reply/route.ts verifies the signature before parsing content, applies durable idempotency -> job.ts runAssistantReplyJob -> own controller with a total deadline -> same generateReply -> drain to a complete reply -> persist.",
          "Job outcome mapping: validation/contract/forbidden -> dead-letter without retry; rate_limited/unavailable -> bounded exponential retry to the attempt cap then dead-letter; unexpected exception -> reported once here since the job is the outer channel, then retry then dead-letter; otherwise complete."
        ],
        "decisions": [
          {
            "topic": "Capability boundary",
            "choice": "One module, src/modules/assistant-reply, covering both the stream and the job",
            "rationale": "Both channels serve one product goal, vocabulary, generation policy, and change authority. A second channel is not a second capability, and a transport- or provider-named module (sse, llm-provider) is a listed failure mode."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Application operation",
            "choice": "Keep application/generate-reply.ts",
            "rationale": "It survives the deletion test: request shaping, delta normalization, and the completed-vs-contract decision would otherwise be duplicated into both the stream runtime and the job runner. It is silent, framework-neutral, and receives the smallest deps object."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Provider seam",
            "choice": "One port, ReplyModel.streamReply(request, {signal}) -> Promise<AsyncIterable<ReplyDelta>>, with one private transport",
            "rationale": "The core must name a streaming model in application language for two production consumers. The awaited acquisition is load-bearing: it makes startup failures pre-commit HTTP outcomes. SDK types, credentials, and provider-error mapping stay in server/provider. The port is not justified by mocks or a hypothetical second provider."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Ownership of commit vs lifecycle",
            "choice": "Route owns status, headers, and the pre-commit mapping; the module's private stream runtime owns the AbortController, idle timer, and in-band failures",
            "rationale": "Route Handlers own HTTP and not product behavior, while the stream channel owns commit state and cancellation. Keeping the controller and timer in a module file makes disconnect and idle behavior testable without an HTTP server."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Cancellation mechanics",
            "choice": "Exactly one channel-owned AbortController, linked from request.signal and from ReadableStream cancel(reason), with its signal passed to the provider",
            "rationale": "Setting a flag or breaking a local loop is not cancellation; the upstream producer must observe the abort, and the test only passes when it does."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Idle timeout shape",
            "choice": "Sliding timer cleared and re-armed per delta, with no total duration cap",
            "rationale": "The requirement is provider liveness, not a limit on successful generation length; only the gap between deltas is bounded."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Report-once ownership",
            "choice": "The outer channel reports (stream runtime for HTTP, job runner for queue) and sets an already-reported marker",
            "rationale": "Trusted surfaces, the application operation, and the transport propagate without reporting. The marker prevents framework instrumentation from double-capturing a rethrown failure."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Failure carriers",
            "choice": "Closed semantic code set in domain/failure.ts, mapped per channel: HTTP status pre-commit, in-band error event post-commit, retry/dead-letter for the job",
            "rationale": "Share failure semantics and provider mapping, not one universal result wrapper; the transport changes while the meaning does not."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Job vs stream liveness",
            "choice": "Job uses a total deadline and never the stream's sliding idle timer",
            "rationale": "Reusing one operation must not make the job inherit stream semantics; a shared rule would have to be an explicitly named liveness policy both channels opt into."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Root surfaces",
            "choice": "Only stream.ts, job.ts, and client.ts",
            "rationale": "Each has a named consumer and establishes a runtime boundary. No RSC read, mutation, or shared query key exists, so rsc.ts, actions.ts, server.ts, and query-cache.ts would be empty ceremony."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Omitted segments and shared roots",
            "choice": "No ui/ segment and no shared/**",
            "rationale": "Presentation is route-private and unreused, and with a single capability nothing meets the two-owner promotion gate; identity is demoted to shared/server only when a second capability needs the same meaning."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Transport for the browser stream",
            "choice": "POST with a fetch body reader rather than EventSource",
            "rationale": "The prompt is a request body, and EventSource cannot POST. It is still a browser-owned read over a Route Handler, not a Server Action."
          }
        ],
        "risks": [
          "Pre-commit correctness depends entirely on the port awaiting real upstream acquisition; if the provider client resolves before the connection is established, first-token failures silently become in-band errors at status 200 instead of a 5xx. Pin with a test against the real client.",
          "SSE framing sits in a module-private encoder while status and headers sit in the route; the split is deliberate but means the wire shape is described in two files. The published event union keeps a second transport possible.",
          "Resume is not designed in. Event ids are published so a future Last-Event-ID resume is possible, but a committed stream must never be replayed as an ordinary retry.",
          "Cleanup must be centralized in one finally on every exit path including refusal; a missed path leaks an idle timer and an abort listener per request.",
          "Provider SDK specifics are intentionally absent: the provider-reference skill failed to load in this session, so no streaming or abort API details are asserted. Verify stream-acquisition and AbortSignal semantics against current provider docs when implementing the transport.",
          "Runtime must be pinned (runtime = 'nodejs', dynamic = 'force-dynamic') if the provider client is not edge-compatible, otherwise the stream can be buffered or the route prerendered.",
          "Double reporting is the easiest regression to introduce here; the report-once assertion must cover the channel and framework instrumentation together, not just the channel.",
          "shared/** is empty by design; the first cross-capability need should demote server/identity.ts deliberately rather than by drift into a utils bucket."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "candidate": "candidate-2",
      "response": {
        "summary": "One capability, src/modules/assistant/, owns the assistant reply-generation product goal. A POST Route Handler at app/api/assistant/reply/route.ts is the HTTP boundary; stream.ts publishes openReplyStream, which owns a single channel AbortController, awaits upstream acquisition before the Response is constructed (so startup failures are genuinely pre-commit HTTP), then emits SSE deltas with a sliding provider-liveness idle timeout, and emits an in-band {type:'error',code} event when failure lands after commit. application/generate-reply.ts holds provider-neutral generation policy shared by the stream and the job through one capability-shaped port; the provider SDK, credentials, payload validation, and provider-to-semantic error mapping stay in the private server/provider transport. job.ts gives the background path its own whole-run deadline and retry/dead-letter classification, so it never inherits stream idle-timeout semantics. Unexpected failures are reported exactly once: by the route before commit, by the stream body after commit, by the job channel for jobs; surfaces, the operation, and adapters stay silent. No persistence, no RSC surface, no actions.ts, no shared/kernel promotion is invented.",
        "tree": [
          {
            "path": "src/modules/assistant/domain/reply-request.ts",
            "purpose": "Pure prompt and output-limit invariants; no provider or framework dependency."
          },
          {
            "path": "src/modules/assistant/domain/reply-event.ts",
            "purpose": "Provider-neutral ReplyDelta plus the StreamEvent union (chunk | error | done) and terminal reasons."
          },
