        "decisions": [
          {
            "topic": "Capability boundary",
            "choice": "One module src/modules/assistant/ owning prompt, reply, delta, done, and generation policy",
            "rationale": "One product goal under one policy and lifecycle. Naming it sse, openai, or chat-api would create a transport- or provider-named module, an explicit failure mode; a second endpoint and a job do not by themselves split a capability."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Stream transport",
            "choice": "POST Route Handler plus a stream.ts surface, not a Server Action",
            "rationale": "A long-lived response is its own channel that owns headers, commit state, cancellation, and in-band failures; Server Actions are mutation boundaries and cannot express any of that."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Application operation",
            "choice": "Keep generate-reply.ts because it owns entitlement/quota policy, model and instruction selection, delta sequencing, and the complete-vs-truncated decision, and is shared by two runtime channels",
            "rationale": "Passes the deletion test on two listed criteria. Stated explicitly in the proposal: if in practice it reduces to forwarding the port call, delete it and let both channels call the server adapter — a forwarding operation is worse than none."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Port grain",
            "choice": "A single role method streamReply(request, { signal }): Promise<AsyncIterable<ReplyDelta>>",
            "rationale": "Capability conversation rather than an SDK or endpoint mirror. Returning a promise of the iterable makes upstream acquisition awaitable, which is what keeps startup failure pre-commit; a one-method function type is valid when one operation needs one capability."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Commit-state discipline",
            "choice": "Await upstream acquisition before constructing or returning the Response",
            "rationale": "Creating a ReadableStream and doing the work in an async start() does not make an asynchronous startup failure pre-commit; once the Response is returned, failure belongs to the body channel and can only be reported in band."
          },
          {
            "topic": "Cancellation",
            "choice": "One channel-owned AbortController; request.signal, the body cancel(reason) callback, and the idle timer all abort it, and only its signal is passed inward to the transport fetch",
